#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228855 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/id #uuid "bf22c16e-fc49-5ba5-9c64-89ee66d0a6e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21636" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), \\, y(0) = 8, \\ x \\geq 0, \\, \\lambda = 6$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. The update formula can be written as $y_{n+1} = \\left(1 - 6\\Delta x\\right)^n y_n$.\n2. The method is stable for $\\mid 1 - 6\\Delta x \\mid < 1$.\n3. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.1$.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 8, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -6y, \\qquad y(0) = 8\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nEuler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n,y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n$y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + (-6y_{n}) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 6y_{n} \\cdot Δx; = (1 - 6Δx)y_{n}$.\nHence, statement I is false.\n- For stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$. Here, we have\n$y_{n}|= (1 - 6Δx)y_{n - 1}; = (1 - 6Δx)((1 - 6Δx)y_{n - 2}); = (1 - 6Δx)^{2}y_{n - 2}; ⋮⋮; = (1 - 6Δx)^{n}y_{0}; = 8(1 - 6Δx)^{n}$.\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $\\left|1 - 6\\Delta x\\right| < 1$, as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n- Expanding the inequality $\\left|1 - 6\\Delta x\\right| < 1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & < 1 - 6Δx < 1 \\\\ -2 & < - 6Δx < 0 \\\\ 0 & < 6Δx < 2 \\\\ 0 & < Δx < \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:Since $\\Delta x = 0.1$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.Since $\\Delta x = 0.2$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228856 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/id #uuid "6d580b90-1fbf-541c-8e63-97bb40ce0f3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21637" :question/problem "For the initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0$, the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$Δy_{n}^{(p)}|= f(x_{n}, y_{n}) \\cdot Δx,; y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(f(x_{n}, y_{n}) + f(x_{n + 1}, y_{n} + Δy_{n}^{(p)})) \\cdot Δx$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation $y'=-\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0, \\lambda = 12$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. The stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2$.\n2. The stability region is $\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac16\\right)^2\\right| < 1$.\n3. The method is absolutely stable.\n4. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15$.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n$." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$, is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -12y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n$y_{n} + Δy_{n}^{(p)}|= y_{n} + f(x_{n}, y_{n}) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx$\nThus,\n$y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(f(x_{n}, y_{n}) + f(x_{n + 1}, y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx)) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(-12y_{n} - 12(y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx)) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(-24y_{n} + 144y_{n} \\cdot Δx) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx + 72y_{n} \\cdot (Δx)^{2}; = [1 - 12Δx + 72(Δx)^{2}]y_{n}$.\nSo, the stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2$. Hence, statement I is true.\n- The method is stable when\n$\\mid R(12Δx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 1 - 12Δx + 72(Δx)^{2} \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mid \\mid < 1$.\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n$\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac1{12}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac12\\right| < 1$.\nHence, statement II is false.\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} & < 1 \\\\ 72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} & < \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} & < \\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{12} < Δx - \\frac{1}{12} & < \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ 0 < Δx & < \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15$, and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228857 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/id #uuid "0575b9de-7811-547e-90f4-e7a431dac733" :question/math-academy-id "e-21638" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda = 1$ is the parameter of the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. $\\Delta x = 0.5$\n2. $\\Delta x = 0.9$\n3. $\\Delta x = 1.2$\n4. $\\Delta x = 1.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\mid R(λΔx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 1 - λΔx \\mid \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n$0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$.\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$.\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda=1$, this region simplifies to\n$0 \\mid < Δx < 2$.\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.5$ satisfies the inequality,\n- $\\Delta x = 0.9$ satisfies the inequality,\n- $\\Delta x = 1.2$ satisfies the inequality, and\n- $\\Delta x = 1.8$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler's method is stable for all given step sizes.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/id #uuid "e522ef7a-a93a-5711-9193-6afb8faa4327" :question/math-academy-id "e-21639" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge–Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n1. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$\n2. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$\n3. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$\n4. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n$R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$, is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(0.6) & |= & 1 - 0.6 + ((0.6)^{2})/(2) - ((0.6)^{3})/(6) + ((0.6)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{2747}{5000} \\mid; = 0.5494 < 1.✓$\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(1.4) & |= & 1 - 1.4 + ((1.4)^{2})/(2) - ((1.4)^{3})/(6) + ((1.4)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{4241}{15000} \\mid; = 0.282… < 1.✓$\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(2.8) & |= & 1 - 2.8 + ((2.8)^{2})/(2) - ((2.8)^{3})/(6) + ((2.8)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{639}{625} \\mid; = 1.0224 > 1.\\cdot$\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$, we have\n$\\begin{vmatrix}R(3.3) & |= & 1 - 3.3 + ((3.3)^{2})/(2) - ((3.3)^{3})/(6) + ((3.3)^{4})/(24)\\end{vmatrix}; = \\mid \\frac{167747}{80000} \\mid; = 2.096… > 1.\\cdot$\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$, so it is not absolutely stable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9de2dad0-6dfc-5bc2-a495-825c6104624c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228028} {:answer/id #uuid "aa87dd29-1796-5044-bbfd-7ac8dc109912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228029} {:answer/id #uuid "fb723cf3-6c5e-5092-93a7-e309f44fd981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228030} {:answer/id #uuid "0233c732-d7c6-5f7e-a072-147688a3b9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228031} {:answer/id #uuid "5fef8ef6-bd51-5f12-b586-2af2b85b6cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fef8ef6-bd51-5f12-b586-2af2b85b6cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228032} :answer-field/id #uuid "98495da8-6d4a-57ef-a57b-7f00e3873485" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228027}] :question/id #uuid "97216a46-237d-5c6e-afbc-0108a663f4c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332530" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ = 5$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. The update formula can be written as $y_{n + 1} = (1 - 5Δx)^{n}y_{n}$.\n2. The method is stable for $\\mid 1 - 5Δx \\mid < 1$.\n3. The method is stable for step size $Δx = 0.2$.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx = 0.25$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6c5017d-05bd-5ac7-b1a2-2f7f07a407df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228036} {:answer/id #uuid "ff55b311-7ec2-592e-b3b0-798f3c4f51d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228037} {:answer/id #uuid "27ad2ffe-ccf5-5b21-9490-6ac500eaa8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228038} {:answer/id #uuid "35890226-ed2e-5dbb-956b-9d2bf206a9e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228039} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c8170-af13-5a3f-9f7a-8d4cf99aff8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6c5017d-05bd-5ac7-b1a2-2f7f07a407df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228036} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67a2019-e5a4-5e81-9df6-963899eadfca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228035}] :question/id #uuid "3afc7fc5-d735-57e5-883a-73d956edd87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332533" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $λ$ is the parameter of the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ > 0$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. $λΔx = 0.67$\n2. $λΔx = 1.20$\n3. $λΔx = 1.80$\n4. $λΔx = 2.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(λΔx) = 1 - λΔx$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c38ab3b8-af3b-5693-a116-987bffcf95eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228070} {:answer/id #uuid "d8766537-e50d-52ef-9009-d59485b6ce1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228071} {:answer/id #uuid "fab31041-9414-5a66-9a81-ea5179965420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228072} {:answer/id #uuid "1090aff5-58ab-5c8f-a2ba-cd000b12aa1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228073} {:answer/id #uuid "15836a4e-d6cf-532e-afa9-272a6c78b47e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8766537-e50d-52ef-9009-d59485b6ce1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228071} :answer-field/id #uuid "40dcf1a2-7dac-5102-9283-9753f5a73553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228069}] :question/id #uuid "ddd8726a-dc80-58fb-bcd2-049555ab4372" :question/math-academy-id "q-332737" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFor which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $λ$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n1. $λΔx = 0.25$\n2. $λΔx = 1$\n3. $λΔx = 1.5$\n4. $λΔx = 9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n$R(λΔ) = \\frac{1}{1 + λΔx}$." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
